      "content": "wow, ok, that's a pretty intense take. it sounds like you're saying the \"scientific\" part is essentially just a cover-up for the social bias.\n\nfor my paper, i need to be able to address the actual data points people bring up. for example, many researchers point to the specific statistical correlations between high test scores and long-term success in university and high-income careers as proof of their utility. \n\nif those correlations are statistically significant and measurable, how would you explain that? is it also just part of the \"facade,\" or is there a different sociological way to look at why the data seems to show that these tests actually do predict something?",
